description:
Write lesson 6.01's detection and conversion, so the editor saves a file exactly the way it found it:

```cpp
enum class Encoding { Utf8, Utf8Bom, Utf16Le, Utf16Be, Legacy };
enum class LineEnding { Crlf, Lf, Cr };
struct Loaded { std::string text; Encoding encoding; LineEnding ending; };   // text: UTF-8 with \n line endings

Loaded decodeFile(const std::string& bytes);
std::string encodeFile(const std::string& text, Encoding encoding, LineEnding ending);
bool isValidUtf8(const std::string& bytes);
```

**decodeFile**
- Encoding: `EF BB BF` at the start is `Utf8Bom`; `FF FE` is `Utf16Le`; `FE FF` is `Utf16Be` (the BOM is not part of the text). Otherwise valid UTF-8 is `Utf8`, and anything else is `Legacy`, decoded as Latin-1 (each byte is the code point of the same value) for this exercise.
- UTF-16 is decoded to UTF-8, including surrogate pairs; an odd final byte is ignored.
- Line ending: count `\r\n`, lone `\r` and lone `\n` in the decoded text; the most common wins, with ties going to `Crlf`, then `Lf`. A text without any line ending is `Crlf` (the Windows default).
- The text is returned with every ending converted to `\n`.

**encodeFile** converts `\n` to the chosen ending, then writes the encoding with its BOM if it has one (`Legacy` writes Latin-1, using `?` for characters above U+00FF).

**isValidUtf8** checks strictly: no overlong forms, no surrogates, nothing above U+10FFFF, no truncated sequence.

